    ('333b1b12-dfb2-4ee3-a3c0-4af360dd498f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Veterans of the Legio VII Claudia settled Scupi by the Vardar River, to archaeologist Mikulčić the largest known Roman settlement in the region, abandoned in AD 518 after an earthquake.', 'A Roman military camp from the 2nd century BC that grew into a major city. It lies beneath modern Skopje — the capital sits on Roman foundations.', 'teaser-card-s017-rollback:333b1b12-dfb2-4ee3-a3c0-4af360dd498f:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s017: card_description ''A Roman military camp from the 2nd century BC that grew into a major city. It lies beneath modern Skopje — the capital sits on Roman foundations.'' restored on Scupi', 'a3eed0c28113c7d11e610a07a3b4ee01434ae4a3fa6472f66b4c0d65051ee0f4|54812cbe5c29ed8fe19f271afe8c4c804fa772a6e79b3c058c33a8474f9bb9e4|54812cbe5c29ed8fe19f271afe8c4c804fa772a6e79b3c058c33a8474f9bb9e4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-007", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Veterans of the Legio VII Claudia settled Scupi by the Vardar River, to archaeologist Mikulčić the largest known Roman settlement in the region, abandoned in AD 518 after an earthquake."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-007 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "veterans of the Legio VII Claudia settled Scupi [S5]; Scupi is by the Vardar River [S1]; according to archaeologist Mikulčić, Scupi was the largest known Roman settlement in the region [S7]; Scupi was abandoned in AD 518 [S3]; the abandonment came after an earthquake [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-019 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Scupi is an archaeological site of a Roman town by the Vardar River near Skopje, North Macedonia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scupi]; Scupi became a Roman colony where many veteran legionnaires were settled [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scupi]; Veterans of the Legio VII Claudia settled Scupi [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scupi]; According to archaeologist Mikulčić, Scupi was the largest known Roman settlement in the region [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scupi]; Scupi was abandoned in AD 518 after an earthquake [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scupi]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('33e2399e-fd68-4aab-bf79-c5695a6c0bf0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Its name possibly joins the Tarma, an ethnic group, with the Quechua word tampu, an inn: Tarmatambo, an archaeological site in the Junín Region, declared National Cultural Heritage in 2002.', 'Inca tambo in the Junín highlands, part of the road-station network that linked the Inca empire''s thousands of kilometres of road. Such way stations supplied travellers, armies, and state runners.', 'teaser-card-s017-rollback:33e2399e-fd68-4aab-bf79-c5695a6c0bf0:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s017: card_description "Inca tambo in the Junín highlands, part of the road-station network that linked the Inca empire''s thousands of kilometres of road. Such way stations supplied travellers, armies, and state runners." restored on Tarmatambo', 'e7a4a217b179d703417c2bb7711a288a49b707558f260b4ff581c3476699da7c|3924e8ae74b500957319a185dbe22ef2cc15192a6ffd31ca62cc54270d5e7b36|3924e8ae74b500957319a185dbe22ef2cc15192a6ffd31ca62cc54270d5e7b36', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-007", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Its name possibly joins the Tarma, an ethnic group, with the Quechua word tampu, an inn: Tarmatambo, an archaeological site in the Junín Region, declared National Cultural Heritage in 2002."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-007 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the name possibly joins Tarma, an ethnic group, with the Quechua word tampu [S1]; Tarma is an ethnic group [S1]; Quechua tampu means inn [S1]; Tarmatambo is an archaeological site [S1]; it is in the Junín Region [S1]; it was declared National Cultural Heritage [S3]; the declaration was in 2002 [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-020 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tarmatambo is an archaeological site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarmatambo]; The site is in the Junín Region (Peru) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarmatambo]; The name is possibly derived from Tarma, an ethnic group (the derivation is uncertain) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarmatambo]; Tarma is an ethnic group [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarmatambo]; The second part of the name is the Quechua word tampu [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarmatambo]; Quechua tampu means an inn [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarmatambo]; The site was declared National Cultural Heritage [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarmatambo]; The declaration as National Cultural Heritage was made in 2002 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarmatambo]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3415ea00-bddf-4f1d-bed2-7ce85b6c5871'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Above Worbarrow Bay, the southern part of Flower''s Barrow, an Iron Age hillfort, is falling into the sea. Hut circles lie within; a 1939 dig found sling-stones near the western entrance.', 'Over 2,500 years old and still crumbling into Worbarrow Bay — erosion has taken a large portion of this clifftop hillfort. What remains is scheduled; what falls is beyond recovery.', 'teaser-card-s017-rollback:3415ea00-bddf-4f1d-bed2-7ce85b6c5871:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s017: card_description ''Over 2,500 years old and still crumbling into Worbarrow Bay — erosion has taken a large portion of this clifftop hillfort. What remains is scheduled; what falls is beyond recovery.'' restored on Flower''s Barrow', '2c53d5e7e5a12502d0b0a0f0c43cb21f9dcf9775e93f7d2e1cd11a9b44f07b90|c62ea93701eceef70ed6b6ed21ac80e564bf0fd35dc6434b42d897fdfaa56b63|c62ea93701eceef70ed6b6ed21ac80e564bf0fd35dc6434b42d897fdfaa56b63', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-007", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Above Worbarrow Bay, the southern part of Flower''s Barrow, an Iron Age hillfort, is falling into the sea. Hut circles lie within; a 1939 dig found sling-stones near the western entrance."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-007 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Flower''s Barrow is above Worbarrow Bay [S1]; Flower''s Barrow is an Iron Age hillfort [S1]; its southern part is falling into the sea [S4]; hut circles lie within the fort [S6]; a dig took place in 1939 [S7]; the 1939 dig found sling-stones [S7]; the sling-stones were found near the western entrance [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-020 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Flower''s Barrow is an Iron Age hillfort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower%27s_Barrow]; The hillfort lies above Worbarrow Bay [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower%27s_Barrow]; The southern part of the hillfort is falling into the sea [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower%27s_Barrow]; Hut circles lie within the hillfort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower%27s_Barrow]; An excavation (dig) took place in 1939 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower%27s_Barrow]; The 1939 dig found sling-stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower%27s_Barrow]; The sling-stones were found near the western entrance [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower%27s_Barrow]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('34f1acbd-b1d2-4349-9946-75a36f323d6b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the Roman route from Lissus through Naissus to Ratiaria, 60 km northeast of Naissus, stands Timacum Minus, the oldest military fort in the Timok Valley. Its counterpart lies near Svrljig.', 'Timacum Minus and its counterpart Timacum Maius are located in the Timok river valley.', 'teaser-card-s017-rollback:34f1acbd-b1d2-4349-9946-75a36f323d6b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s017: card_description ''Timacum Minus and its counterpart Timacum Maius are located in the Timok river valley.'' restored on Timacum Minus', 'e50758a3ca5667a61d7ca6e5a5a62c9008a56ae6c7128b2a0965636dd3c51e6e|83345085fde44e8898b671a1a90c4071159029019d31cf2bf99ca4540913efd4|83345085fde44e8898b671a1a90c4071159029019d31cf2bf99ca4540913efd4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-007", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Roman route from Lissus through Naissus to Ratiaria, 60 km northeast of Naissus, stands Timacum Minus, the oldest military fort in the Timok Valley. Its counterpart lies near Svrljig."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-007 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Timacum Minus lies on the Roman route from Lissus through Naissus to Ratiaria [S3]; it is 60 km northeast of Naissus [S4]; Timacum Minus is a military fort [S1]; it is the oldest military fort in the Timok Valley [S1]; its counterpart lies near Svrljig [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-020 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Timacum Minus is a military fort (an archaeological site) in Serbia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timacum_Minus]; It lies on the Roman route from Lissus through